3.2 Six Critical Rules for Writing Bug-Free .ten Scores
Strict Measure Beat Math (4.0 Beats per 4/4 Bar):
:1 = 4.0 beats (whole note)
:2. = 3.0 beats (dotted half note)
:2 = 2.0 beats (half note)
:4. = 1.5 beats (dotted quarter note)
:4 = 1.0 beat (quarter note)
:8. = 0.75 beats (dotted eighth note)
:8 = 0.5 beats (eighth note)
:16 = 0.25 beats (sixteenth note)
:32 = 0.125 beats (thirty-second note)
Every track line (and every voice inside <[ v1: ... | v2: ... ]>) inside a measure N block must sum to 4.0 quarter-note beats in 4/4 time.
The Double-Dot (..) Rule on Dotted Notes with Modifiers:
When a dotted note (:2., :4., :8.) is followed by a modifier (.f, .vib, .zap), you MUST write two dots (..): the first dot is the rhythmic dot and the second dot is the modifier separator!
Correct: c#5:2..mp.vib (3.0 beats + f#5:4.mp.legato 1.0 beat = 4.0 beats).
Wrong: c#5:2.mp.vib (parsed as a 2.0-beat half note with .mp.vib!).
Stateless Modifiers vs. Sticky Pitch/Duration:
Octave numbers (c4 d e) and durations (c4:8 d e) are sticky across a voice.
Modifiers (.p, .mf, .ff, .ped, .stacc, .legato, .detune(...)) are stateless and apply only to the token they are attached to. Always write dynamics (.mp, .f, .ff) and .ped on every note where you want them active.
Playable Bass & Cello Registers (MIDI >= 21):
SoundFont banks clamp at A0 (MIDI 21). Never write cb (Contrabass) notes below a0 (keep cb in C1–G2, e.g. c1, d1, f1, f#1, g1, a1) and keep vc (Cello) in C2–C4 (c2, f#2, g2).
On style=synth tracks (reese_bass, supersaw), f#1 or d1 automatically engages the phase-locked sub-oscillator one octave lower (f#0 / d0), so f#1–c2 gives maximum sub-bass weight.
Pitched Orchestral Timpani vs. Drum Kit Grid:
Define timp as style=standard patch="timpani" so you can write real tuned timpani pitches (f#2:4.ff, c#2:2.ff.roll(12)) that match each measure's harmony.
Define drums / perc as style=grid patch="tenuto_808_kit" using trigger keys: k (kick), s (snare), cp (clap), rim (rimshot), ch / h (closed hat — chokes open hat), oh (open hat), lt/mt/ht (toms), cr (crash), rd (ride).
Never put a trailing | pipe outside the closing ]> of a polyphonic block (<[ v1: ... | v2: ... ]>).
Mid-Piece Tempo Changes (meta @{ tempo: N }):
Place meta @{ tempo: 88 } as the first line inside any measure N { ... } block to smoothly transition the score's tempo and playhead synchronization at that exact bar.
